---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import { loadCatalogue, PIN } from '../lib/catalogue'

const items = loadCatalogue()
---

<Base
  title="About: Beamish"
  description="Beamish, adj. Shining brightly, radiant. First recorded in 1530. A library of light and glow effects named after a word that is nearly five hundred years old."
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-[62ch] px-5 pt-32 pb-16 md:px-8 md:pt-44">
    <h1 class="text-[clamp(2.2rem,7vw,4rem)] leading-[0.95]">
      The word is 496 years old.
    </h1>

    <div
      class="text-l2 mt-10 grid gap-5 font-display text-[1.05rem] leading-relaxed"
    >
      <p>
        <strong class="text-l1">Beamish</strong>, <em>adj.</em> Shining brightly,
        radiant. It turns up first in 1530, in John Palsgrave's
        <em>Lesclarcissement de la langue francoyse</em>, the earliest
        French-English dictionary, glossing the French <em>radieux</em> as
        “Beamysshe as the sonne is”.
      </p>
      <p>
        Lewis Carroll used it in <em>Jabberwocky</em> 341 years later, alongside
        <em>chortle</em> and <em>galumph</em>, and seems to have thought he had
        coined all three. He coined two of them.
      </p>
      <p>It is also my surname, which is the rest of the reason.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="border-line mt-16 border-b pb-3 text-[1.05rem]">How it works</h2>
    <div class="text-l2 mt-5 grid gap-4 text-[0.9rem]">
      <p>
        There is no package. Every item here has a <strong class="text-l1"
          >Copy prompt</strong
        > button that gives your agent two source URLs, every option with the range
        that actually looks good, wiring for React, Vue and plain HTML, cleanup and
        SSR notes, and the three mistakes most likely to be made with that effect.
        The agent then adapts all of it to the project in front of it, which is the
        part a package cannot do.
      </p>
      <p>
        If your agent cannot reach the web, the second button gives the same
        instructions with the source pasted in. Nobody should hit a dead end
        because their tool cannot fetch a URL.
      </p>
      <p>
        Prompt URLs pin to a git tag, currently <code class="text-l1">{PIN}</code
        >, and never to <code>main</code>. A prompt you paste today still fetches
        the file it was written about in a year.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="border-line mt-16 border-b pb-3 text-[1.05rem]">Built for paper</h2>
    <div class="text-l2 mt-5 grid gap-4 text-[0.9rem]">
      <p>
        Every library in this space assumes a near-black canvas, and their effects
        come apart the moment you put them on anything else. All {items.length} items
        here work the other way round: the background is
        <code class="text-l1">rgb(251, 250, 244)</code>, a warm off-white, and each
        effect is built to look right on it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Light on light is the harder problem. It is also the more useful one,
        because most of the web is not black.
      </p>
      <p>
        Everything is MIT. Where an effect is recognisably derived from a specific
        published demo, the credit is on its page.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
